.SH "NAME"
git-wts \- Manage a worktree project layout and sync local files across worktrees
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.sp
.nf
\fBgit\fR \fBwts\fR \fBclone\fR \fI<origin>\fR [\fI<dir>\fR]
\fBgit\fR \fBwts\fR \fBadd\fR [\fB\-u\fR] [\fI<path>\fR\&...\:]
\fBgit\fR \fBwts\fR \fBcommit\fR [\fB\-m\fR \fI<message>\fR]
\fBgit\fR \fBwts\fR \fBpush\fR
\fBgit\fR \fBwts\fR \fBpull\fR [\fB\-r\fR]
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
Set up a project directory for development with \fBgit-worktree\fR(1), and keep local files that the project does not track (such as \fB\&.env\fR or \fB\&.idea/\fR) in sync between its worktrees\&.
.sp
A project directory holds a bare clone of the project repository and any number of linked worktrees created with \fBgit\fR \fBworktree\fR \fBadd\fR\&. Next to it lives a second, private repository, the \fIhub\fR, which version controls the local files\&. Each worktree gets its own \fB\&.worktreesync\fR git directory that tracks the hub, so local files are staged, committed, pushed and pulled much like project files, using the \fBgit\fR \fBwts\fR commands below\&.
.sp
Apart from \fBclone\fR, every command acts on the \fB\&.worktreesync\fR of the worktree containing the current directory, and can be run from any subdirectory of it\&.
.SH "COMMANDS"
.PP
\fBclone\fR \fI<origin>\fR [\fI<dir>\fR]
.RS 4
Create the project directory
\fI<dir>\fR
for
\fI<origin>\fR
(see "LAYOUT" below)\&. The project repository is cloned bare into
\fB\&.remote\&.bare\fR, its fetch refspec is changed to
\fB+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*\fR
so that branches are tracked as remote\-tracking branches, and it is fetched again\&. The hub is created with an initial commit containing
\fB\&.worktreeinclude\fR, and the hooks described in "HOOKS" are installed\&.
.sp
If
\fI<dir>\fR
is omitted, it is derived from
\fI<origin>\fR
the same way
\fBgit-clone\fR(1)
does it: trailing slashes and a trailing
\fB/\&.git\fR
or
\fB\&.git\fR
are removed, and the last path component is used, so both
\fBgit@host:me/foo\&.git\fR
and
\fB/src/foo/\&.git\fR
give
\fBfoo\fR\&.
\fI<dir>\fR
may already exist only if it is empty\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBadd\fR [\fB\-u\fR] [\fI<path>\fR\&...\:]
.RS 4
Stage each
\fI<path>\fR
in the worktree\(cqs
\fB\&.worktreesync\fR\&. Paths may be files or directories and are relative to the current directory\&. All paths are validated before anything is changed\&.
.sp
Each path is also recorded as an anchored entry (for example
\fB/src/local\&.cfg\fR) in the worktree\(cqs
\fB\&.worktreeinclude\fR
and in the project repository\(cqs
\fBinfo/exclude\fR, unless an entry for it is already present with or without the leading or trailing slash\&. Since
\fBinfo/exclude\fR
lives in the bare repository, the exclusion applies to every worktree\&.
\fB\&.worktreeinclude\fR
is staged when it changes\&.
.sp
Paths are staged with
\fBgit\fR \fBadd\fR \fB\-\-force\fR, as local files are commonly ignored by the project\(cqs
\fB\&.gitignore\fR\&. For a directory this includes files ignored by a
\fB\&.gitignore\fR
inside it\&.
.sp
\fBadd\fR
refuses paths tracked by the project repository, the worktree root,
\fB\&.git\fR,
\fB\&.worktreesync\fR, and paths outside the current worktree\&. At least one
\fI<path>\fR
or
\fB\-u\fR
must be given\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBcommit\fR [\fB\-m\fR \fI<message>\fR]
.RS 4
Commit the changes staged in the worktree\(cqs
\fB\&.worktreesync\fR\&. Without
\fB\-m\fR
an editor is opened as for
\fBgit-commit\fR(1)\&. The user\(cqs git configuration applies, including commit signing\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBpush\fR
.RS 4
Push the worktree\(cqs
\fB\&.worktreesync\fR
commits to the hub\&. If another worktree pushed first, the push is rejected; integrate its changes with
\fBgit\fR \fBwts\fR \fBpull\fR \fB\-r\fR
and push again\&. A successful push updates the other worktrees, see "HOOKS"\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBpull\fR [\fB\-r\fR]
.RS 4
Fetch from the hub and integrate its commits into the worktree\(cqs
\fB\&.worktreesync\fR\&. Without
\fB\-r\fR, divergent histories are handled according to the
\fBpull\&.*\fR
settings in
\fBgit-config\fR(1); with
\fBpull\&.ff=only\fR
a pull after a rejected push fails, and
\fB\-r\fR
is needed\&.
.sp
As with
\fBgit-pull\fR(1), uncommitted changes to tracked files make a rebasing pull fail; commit them first\&.
.RE
.SH "OPTIONS"
.PP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
.RS 4
Show a usage summary and exit\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-update\fR
.RS 4
With
\fBadd\fR: also stage changes to files already tracked by
\fB\&.worktreesync\fR, including deletions, and new files inside directories listed in
\fB\&.worktreeinclude\fR\&. May be combined with
\fI<path>\fR
arguments\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-m\fR \fI<message>\fR, \fB\-\-message\fR=\fI<message>\fR
.RS 4
With
\fBcommit\fR: use
\fI<message>\fR
as the commit message instead of opening an editor\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-rebase\fR
.RS 4
With
\fBpull\fR: rebase local commits on top of the hub instead of following the
\fBpull\&.*\fR
configuration\&.
.RE
.sp
Options are rejected by commands they do not apply to\&.
.SH "LAYOUT"
.sp
After \fBgit\fR \fBwts\fR \fBclone\fR \fI<origin>\fR \fIproject\fR and \fBgit\fR \fBworktree\fR \fBadd\fR \fIfeat\fR, the project directory contains:
.PP
\fBproject/\&.remote\&.bare\fR
.RS 4
Bare clone of
\fI<origin>\fR, shared by all worktrees\&.
\fBinfo/exclude\fR
lists
\fB\&.worktreesync\fR,
\fB\&.worktreeinclude\fR
and every path added with
\fBgit\fR \fBwts\fR \fBadd\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBproject/\&.git\fR
.RS 4
A gitfile containing
\fBgitdir: \&./\&.remote\&.bare\fR, so that git commands such as
\fBgit\fR \fBworktree\fR \fBadd\fR
and
\fBgit\fR \fBfetch\fR
work from the project directory\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBproject/\&.worktreesync\&.bare\fR
.RS 4
The hub: a bare repository whose
\fBmaster\fR
branch holds the synced local files\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBproject/\&.worktreesync\fR
.RS 4
A regular clone of the hub, for inspecting or editing its contents from the project directory\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBproject/feat/\&.worktreesync\fR
.RS 4
The worktree\(cqs sync repository: a git directory whose work tree is the worktree itself (\fBcore\&.worktree=\&.\&.\fR), with
\fBstatus\&.showUntrackedFiles=no\fR
to hide project files, and a
\fBmaster\fR
branch tracking the hub\(cqs\&. It can be used directly with
\fBgit\fR \fB\-\-git\-dir=\&.worktreesync\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBproject/feat/\&.worktreeinclude\fR
.RS 4
The list of synced paths, in
\fBgitignore\fR(5)
syntax, itself tracked by the hub\&.
.RE
.SH "HOOKS"
.sp
\fBclone\fR installs every file in \fBhooks/\fR\fINAME\fR\fB/\fR, found next to the (symlink\-resolved) \fBgit\-wts\fR script, into the hooks directory of \fB\&.\fR\fINAME\fR in the project directory:
.PP
\fBpost\-checkout\fR (in \fB\&.remote\&.bare\fR)
.RS 4
When
\fBgit\fR \fBworktree\fR \fBadd\fR
creates a worktree, set up its
\fB\&.worktreesync\fR
and check out the hub\(cqs
\fBmaster\fR
into it, bringing in all synced files\&. Worktrees that already contain a
\fB\&.worktreesync\fR
are left alone\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBpost\-receive\fR (in \fB\&.worktreesync\&.bare\fR)
.RS 4
When a push moves the hub\(cqs
\fBmaster\fR, fast\-forward the project directory\(cqs
\fB\&.worktreesync\fR
clone and the
\fB\&.worktreesync\fR
of every other worktree\&. A worktree is skipped, and never merged, if it has unpushed commits, is not on
\fBmaster\fR
(for example during a rebase), or has uncommitted changes the update would overwrite\&. One line per worktree is reported in the output of the push:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
remote: worktreesync: updated (project root)
remote: worktreesync: skipped b (unpushed commits, run git\-wts pull \-r)
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.RE
.sp
Projects created before a hook was added can install it by copying it into place and making it executable\&.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.sp
Set up a project and share a local environment file and IDE settings with every worktree:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
$ git wts clone git@github\&.com:me/foo\&.git
$ cd foo
$ git worktree add feat
$ cd feat
$ git wts add \&.env \&.idea
$ git wts commit \-m \*(Aqshare local settings\*(Aq
$ git wts push
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
Worktrees created afterwards check out \fB\&.env\fR and \fB\&.idea/\fR automatically\&. To publish later changes to them:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
$ git wts add \-u
$ git wts commit \-m \*(Aqupdate run configurations\*(Aq
$ git wts push
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
If the push is rejected because another worktree pushed first, rebase onto the hub and push again\&. When both changed the same file, resolve the conflict and finish the rebase with git directly:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
$ git wts pull \-r
CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in \&.env
$ $EDITOR \&.env
$ git wts add \&.env
$ git \-\-git\-dir=\&.worktreesync rebase \-\-continue
$ git wts push
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.SH "BUGS"
.sp
\fBadd\fR, \fBcommit\fR, \fBpush\fR and \fBpull\fR only work inside a linked worktree; the \fB\&.worktreesync\fR clone in the project directory is managed with plain git\&.
.sp
There is no command to continue or abort a rebase started by \fBpull\fR \fB\-r\fR; use \fBgit\fR \fB\-\-git\-dir=\&.worktreesync\fR \fBrebase\fR \fB\-\-continue\fR or \fB\-\-abort\fR\&.
.sp
Removing a synced file stages its deletion with \fBadd\fR \fB\-u\fR, but leaves its entries in \fB\&.worktreeinclude\fR and \fBinfo/exclude\fR\&.
.sp
Because untracked files are hidden in \fB\&.worktreesync\fR, git\(cqs status hints mention \fB\-u\fR; this refers to \fBgit\fR \fBstatus\fR \fB\-u\fR, not \fBgit\fR \fBwts\fR \fB\-u\fR\&.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.sp
\fBgit-worktree\fR(1), \fBgit-clone\fR(1), \fBgit-pull\fR(1), \fBgithooks\fR(5), \fBgitignore\fR(5)
